    "font" : "LMRoman10-Regular",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In this section, we address numerical algorithms for the solution of (Pw)."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H2",
    "id" : 35,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 89.291, 409.319, 224.728, 426.427 ],
    "heading level" : 2,
    "font" : "LMSans10-Bold",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "4.1 Fixed-point iteration"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 36,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 88.888, 307.816, 507.5, 404.571 ],
    "font" : "LMRoman10-Regular",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "As an optimality condition for (Pw) we derived r(w) = 0, cf. Theorem 2.6. This can be transformed into the fixed-point equation w = w+τr(w) with τ > 0. A possible algorithm for the solution is the fixed-point iteration wk+1 := wk + τkr(wk) with appropriate step sizes τk > 0. It is not clear, how to choose these step sizes. We will see in Lemma 4.2 below, that the direction r(wk) is a descent direction for J. However, since J is not C1, Armijo step sizes could become arbitrarily small and the usual convergence proof does not work. Similarly, constant (but small) step sizes might not give a decrease of J."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 37,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 100.2, 294.267, 507.5, 309.726 ],
    "font" : "LMRoman10-Regular",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Instead, we can view the above iteration scheme as a discretization of an ODE. Indeed,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 38,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 89.291, 267.169, 506.203, 296.177 ],
    "font" : "LMRoman10-Regular",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "the above update formula yields (wk+1 − wk)/τk = r(wk). This is an explicit Euler scheme for the ODE"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 39,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 260.069, 228.666, 335.207, 261.462 ],
    "font" : "LMRoman10-Regular",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "w′(t) = r(w(t)), w(0) = w0."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 40,
